              "text": "Whether you are trying to conceive or trying to rule out a pregnancy, interpreting home pregnancy test results can sometimes feel confusing. Home pregnancy tests work by detecting **human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG)**, a hormone produced by the placenta shortly after a fertilized egg implants in the uterus. \n\n### 1. How to Read Your Pregnancy Test Results\nMost tests require you to wait 3 to 5 minutes before reading the result window. Because instructions vary by brand, always check the package insert first.\n\n*   **Standard Line Tests:** These tests display one or two lines.\n    *   **The Control Line (C):** This line must always appear. It proves the test strip has successfully absorbed the urine and is functioning properly. If no control line appears, the test is invalid, and you must take a new one.\n    *   **The Test Line (T):** This line only appears if hCG is detected. \n        *   *One line (Control only):* Negative (not pregnant).\n        *   *Two lines (Control + Test):* Positive (pregnant), even if the test line is very faint.\n*   **Symbol/Plus Sign Tests:** These tests show a single line (negative) or a plus sign (positive). \n*   **Digital Tests:** These screens display clear text such as \"Pregnant\" or \"Not Pregnant\" (or \"+\" and \"-\"), eliminating the need to squint at faint lines.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Is It a Faint Positive or an Evaporation Line?\nOne of the most common points of confusion is a barely visible second line. \n\n*   **A Faint Positive:** If the second line **has color** (pink or blue, depending on the dye used in your test) and appeared **within the recommended reading window** (usually 3\u20135 minutes), it is highly likely a positive result. In the very early stages of pregnancy, hCG levels are still quite low, resulting in a faint line.\n*   **An Evaporation Line:** If the second line is **colorless, grey, or looks like a shadow**, and only appeared **after the test dried** (outside the recommended reading window), it is an evaporation line. This is caused by urine evaporating off the test strip and does not indicate pregnancy. \n\n---\n\n### 3. Understanding False Results\nWhile home pregnancy tests are highly accurate (around 97% to 99% when used correctly), false results can happen.\n\n#### False Negatives (More Common)\nA test may tell you that you are not pregnant when you actually are. This typically occurs due to:\n*   **Testing too early:** Testing before your missed period, or before hCG levels have built up enough to be detected.\n*   **Diluted urine:** Drinking large amounts of water right before testing can dilute the concentration of hCG. For the most accurate result, **test first thing in the morning**, when your urine is most concentrated.\n*   **Reading the test too early:** Not giving the test enough time to react.\n\n#### False Positives (Very Rare)\nA test may tell you that you are pregnant when you are not. This is rare because tests only react if hCG is present, but it can happen due to:\n*   **Chemical pregnancy:** An early pregnancy loss that occurs shortly after implantation.\n*   **Recent pregnancy or miscarriage:** hCG can remain in your bloodstream and urine for several weeks after a pregnancy ends.\n*   **Medications:** Certain fertility drugs containing hCG can cause false positives (most other medications, including birth control pills or pain relievers, do not affect results).\n*   **Medical conditions:** Rare ovarian cysts or certain hormone-producing disorders can elevate hCG levels.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Next Steps Based on Your Results\n\n*   **If your test is Positive:** Contact a primary care physician or an OB/GYN to schedule an appointment. They can confirm the pregnancy with a highly sensitive clinical blood test and begin discussing your prenatal care.\n*   **If your test is Negative, but your period is still late:** Wait **48 to 72 hours and test again**. Since hCG levels double approximately every two days in early pregnancy, a repeat test will provide a much clearer answer. If your period continues to be absent and subsequent tests remain negative, consult your doctor to evaluate other potential causes for a missed cycle (such as stress, thyroid issues, or hormonal imbalances).\n*   **If your test is Invalid (no control line):** Discard the test and try again with a brand-new kit. Ensure you follow the timing and collection instructions exactly as written.",
